# Create a game

Choose your race, the galaxy you will explore, and the players who will share it. You can play against AI opponents, control multiple races yourself, or host a game for other people.

## First game

1. Open [Single Player](/single-player-game).
2. Choose your race in the **Host** field. If you have no saved races, you can use the Humanoids.
3. Give the game a name. For a first game, Small size, Normal density, and Moderate player positions are a useful starting point.
4. Leave the two AI opponents on Normal, or choose Easy for a gentler start. Remove an opponent with its remove button, or add another using the **Players** heading.
5. Review the victory conditions and select **Create Game**.

A single-player game generates its galaxy immediately. Creating a game with open or guest slots takes you to setup so the other players can choose their races before you start.

Want to experiment first? [Try the interactive example](#try-it) below. It works without signing in.

## Choose a race

The Host field lists your saved races. Use [Races](/races) to create or edit a race before starting a game. A race defines your abilities, preferred environments, population growth, economy, and research costs.

Your race must fit within the available advantage points. The race editor calculates these as you change traits and settings; an overspent race cannot be saved.

Creating a game uses a copy of the selected race. Editing your saved race later does not change a race already in a game.

## Galaxy settings

| Setting | What it changes |
| --- | --- |
| Name | The name shown in your game list. |
| Size | The dimensions of the galaxy. Wide variants change its shape. Larger galaxies generally mean more travel and a longer game. |
| Density | How many planets populate the galaxy. Consider this together with galaxy size and player count. |
| Player Positions | How close together homeworlds are placed at the start. |
| Public | Whether other users can discover and join a game with open slots. A private game uses an invitation link. |
| Random Events | Whether random events can occur during play. |
| Public Player Scores | Whether players can see other players' scores. This is separate from whether the game is public. |
| Beginner: Max Minerals | Increases mineral availability when generating the galaxy. |
| Computer Players Form Alliances | Starts AI players allied with one another. |
| Galaxy Clumping | Groups planets into clusters instead of distributing them evenly. |
| Start Mode | Normal, Accelerated BBS Play, or Max Start. |

**Normal** uses the standard starting conditions. **Accelerated BBS Play** gives a stronger beginning, including extra homeworld surface minerals and starting population. **Max Start** begins with maximum technology and abundant starting resources for trying advanced play.

The form below starts with the same defaults as the real creation pages. Change size, density, or a checkbox and use Reset to restore them.

## Players and AI

The first slot belongs to you, the host. Additional slots can be:

- **Host:** another race controlled by your account, for hot-seat play. Choose its race separately.
- **AI:** a computer-controlled opponent with its own difficulty setting.
- **Open:** a slot another signed-in player can join.
- **Guest:** a slot for a player invited through a guest sign-in link.

Open [Host Game](/host-game) to begin with a host slot, an open slot, and an AI opponent. You can change that mix before creating the game. Guests can create single-player games, but cannot host multiplayer games.

| AI level | What to expect |
| --- | --- |
| Easy | Slower expansion and later attacks, leaving more time to establish your economy. |
| Normal | The baseline AI strategy. |
| Hard | Faster expansion, earlier attacks, and more deliberate research toward its racial strengths. |
| Cheater | Hard's strategy with a race from a stronger pool that exceeds the normal race-point budget. |

## Victory conditions

Enable the criteria you want the game to use, then adjust their thresholds. You can win through planet ownership, technology, score, production capacity, capital ships, or the highest score after a specified number of years.

**Winner must meet** controls how many enabled criteria a player needs to satisfy. **At least … years must pass** sets the earliest a winner can be declared. Choose a required count no greater than the number of enabled criteria.

For example, requiring one of three enabled conditions allows any of those three routes to victory. Requiring all three makes the objectives cumulative.

## After creation

For single-player games, the galaxy is generated and you can begin issuing orders. Games where one account controls all human races also count as single player.

For multiplayer games, the setup page shows which players are ready. As host, you can update settings, manage slots, and copy invitation links. A private-game invitation lets a signed-in player join an open slot; a guest invitation signs in as that particular guest player. Treat guest links as access to that player's seat.

Once every player is ready, the host can select **Start Game**. Galaxy generation settings such as size and density are chosen before the game starts.

## Try it

This is the real game creation form, using local example races. Switch between Single player and Host a game, add or remove slots, and change the victory conditions. **Preview setup** shows a summary; it does not create a game or save a race. **Reset** restores the selected example's defaults.

<GameCreationExample />

When you are ready, [create a single-player game](/single-player-game) or [host a game](/host-game). You will sign in before creating a real game.
